# BFF server config — SECRETS LIVE HERE, never in any client bundle.
PORT=8080
# 'memory' = runnable in-memory adapter (default). 'odoo' = real Odoo 19 (TODO).
DATA_SOURCE=memory

# NODE_ENV=production makes the server REFUSE TO BOOT on any of the development
# secrets below (see insecureBootReasons() in src/config.ts).
NODE_ENV=development

# Member auth (JWT). Production requires >= 32 characters and not this value.
#   openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-secret
JWT_TTL=30d

# Short-lived signed POS scan token. It REPLACED the static replayable QR on
# 2026-09-08 — almond-app now fetches every barcode from POST /v1/pos/token and
# can no longer build one (see packages/shared/src/pos/tokenWire.ts).
POS_TOKEN_SECRET=change-me-too
# Optional. Unset, the TTL is config.POS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS from
# packages/shared/src/config — the one number the BFF mints with and the app's
# mock reports. Setting it here wins, and needs no app release: the phone reads
# the lifetime off each response (`expiresIn`), never off its own constant.
# Below ~10s the phone's polling floor bites and the member sees a brief
# "code expired" panel between codes (almond-app/lib/posQr.ts).
POS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=60

# Shared key every till presents as `x-pos-key` on /v1/pos/scan, /v1/pos/earn,
# /v1/pos/earn/reverse and /v1/pos/redemption/settle. REQUIRED, >= 32 characters
# in production (the server refuses to boot otherwise). Every route fails
# closed: empty disables them rather than opening them — which is what the old
# `if (POS_SCAN_KEY && ...)` guard did instead. A wrong key answers 401
# `pos_key_invalid` (a misconfigured till — alert operations).
#   openssl rand -base64 48
POS_SCAN_KEY=

# ── The till's earn (POST /v1/pos/earn) ─────────────────────────────────────
# /v1/pos/scan hands the till a signed, single-use EARN TICKET for the member
# whose QR it read; /v1/pos/earn spends it on ONE paid POS order. Owner
# 2026-09-23: points only after the payment is confirmed — the till IS that
# confirmation for an in-store sale.
#
# How long a ticket stays DELIVERABLE. Default 7 days (604800): the Odoo POS
# addon queues /v1/pos/earn in an outbox and retries through an API outage; a
# ticket that died before the outbox drained would silently lose the member's
# points. Single use and signed regardless of its lifetime.
POS_EARN_TICKET_TTL_SECONDS=604800
# The sale must have been PAID close to the scan, however late it is delivered:
# no later than this after the scan (default 6 h) …
POS_EARN_SALE_WINDOW_SECONDS=21600
# … and no earlier than this before it (default 30 min: paid, then showed the QR;
# plus till-clock skew). Outside the window: 400 `paid_at_outside_ticket_window`.
POS_EARN_PAID_BEFORE_SCAN_SECONDS=1800

# ── Points as a tender at the till (POST /v1/pos/points/spend) ──────────────
# ONE member code, scanned once per visit: /v1/pos/scan also hands the till a
# SPEND TICKET. Spending must follow a fresh scan — default 15 minutes (900).
# POS_SPEND_TICKET_TTL_SECONDS=900
# The most points one sale may take (1 point = 1 qirsh). Default 10000 = 100 JOD;
# above it: 400 `points_over_sale_cap`.
# POS_SPEND_MAX_POINTS_PER_SALE=10000

# ── Rate limits (per instance, in process) ──────────────────────────────────
# All optional; the defaults are in src/config.ts. The till routes are limited
# per TILL (source address — see TRUST_PROXY) AND per POS KEY (one key, so a
# chain-wide ceiling: it is what bounds guessing 8-character redemption codes
# through /settle with a leaked key). Earning and settling have separate budgets.
# RATE_POS_EARN_PER_TILL=120        # /earn + /earn/reverse, per till, per minute
# RATE_POS_EARN_PER_KEY=1200        # /earn + /earn/reverse, whole chain, per minute
# RATE_POS_SETTLE_PER_TILL=60       # /redemption/settle, per till, per minute
# RATE_POS_SETTLE_PER_KEY=300       # /redemption/settle, whole chain, per minute
# RATE_POS_SPEND_PER_TILL=60        # /points/spend + its reverse, per till, per minute
# RATE_POS_SPEND_PER_KEY=600        # /points/spend + its reverse, whole chain, per minute
# RATE_POS_IDENTIFY_PER_TILL=30     # /identify (phone typed on the tablet), per till, per minute
# RATE_POS_IDENTIFY_PER_KEY=300     # /identify, whole chain, per minute — bounds number-walking
# RATE_PAYMENT_INTENT_PER_MEMBER=20 # /v1/payments/intent, per member, per minute
# RATE_OTP_REQUEST_PER_IP=20        # sign-in SMS requests, per IP, per 10 minutes
# RATE_OTP_FAILED_VERIFY_PER_IP=20  # wrong codes, per IP, per 15 minutes
# RATE_REDEEM_PER_MEMBER=10
# RATE_SETTLE_PER_MEMBER=10
# RATE_QUOTE_PER_MEMBER=60
# RATE_STOCKOUT_PER_MEMBER=20
# RATE_TRANSFER_PREVIEW_PER_MEMBER=10 # /v1/me/transfers/preview — "whose phone is this?", per member, per minute
# RATE_TRANSFER_PER_MEMBER=10         # /v1/me/transfers — the DAILY CAP bounds the money (packages/shared config); this bounds the rate
# RATE_REFERRAL_ATTACH_PER_MEMBER=10  # /v1/me/referral/attach — makes guessing a 6-character code pointless

# Shared key the BACK OFFICE presents as `x-admin-key`. REQUIRED in production:
# the server refuses to boot without it (insecureBootReasons), and the guard —
# like /v1/pos/scan's — fails closed, so an empty value is a locked door rather
# than an open one.
#
# It gates the corporate register (who pays half price) AND the analytics export
# (/v1/analytics/order-lines, which carries other members' order history and was
# guarded by a plain member token until 2026-09-09).
#
# 🔴 It belongs on a SERVER. almond-web holds it as ADMIN_KEY inside its
# src/app/api/admin/* route handlers; the browser calls those, they call us.
ADMIN_KEY=

# Phone verification. There is deliberately NO fixed code: `OTP_DEV_CODE=123456`
# used to live here and verified EVERY account, whether or not a code had been
# requested. See docs/LOYALTY-ODOO-ARCHITECTURE.md §G gate 0.
#
# Which SMS provider texts the code (src/auth/sms.ts — implement one from
# src/auth/providers/TEMPLATE.ts and register it there).
#   unset, development  → `log`: nothing is texted; the code is written to the
#                          server log as `DEV OTP issued` (how developers, the
#                          E2E suite and the load test sign in)
#   unset, production   → /v1/auth/otp/request answers 503 `sms_unavailable`
#                          (honest — it used to answer `{sent:true}` for a text
#                          nobody sent). Nobody can sign in until one is set.
#   `log` in production → REFUSED AT BOOT (it would log every member's code)
#   a name not registered in src/auth/sms.ts → refused at boot, every environment
# A provider failure answers 502 `sms_failed` and does not count against the
# member's send caps.
SMS_PROVIDER=
# The provider's own settings — placeholders for the TEMPLATE; names are the
# implementer's to choose. SECRETS: server only.
# SMS_API_URL=https://sms-provider.example/api/send
# SMS_API_KEY=
# SMS_SENDER_ID=Almond          # must be REGISTERED with the TRC (via the provider)
# The text. `{code}` is replaced by the code; the server refuses to boot if it
# is missing. Arabic first, one Unicode SMS part (≤ 70 characters).
# The default, left commented so no env-file loader keeps quotes around it:
# OTP_SMS_TEMPLATE=رمز التحقق من ألموند: {code}
OTP_TTL_SECONDS=300
OTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
OTP_RESEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=30
OTP_MAX_SENDS_PER_HOUR=5

# Comma-separated allowed web/app origins (CORS). '*' is for development ONLY:
# production refuses to boot on it — name the front-ends, e.g.
#   CORS_ORIGINS=https://almond.jo,https://admin.almond.jo
CORS_ORIGINS=*

# Where clients' addresses come from — the per-IP sign-in limits and the
# per-till POS limits key on it. REQUIRED in production (the server refuses to
# boot with it unset, because unset and "false" parse alike but only one is a
# decision):
#   true (or a hop count, e.g. 1) — behind a load balancer or PaaS router
#                                   (Render, Fly, Railway, a cloud LB). Unset
#                                   there, EVERY member shares the balancer's
#                                   address and the whole app gets one IP's
#                                   sign-in budget.
#   false                         — ONLY when this server faces the internet
#                                   directly; otherwise X-Forwarded-For is the
#                                   caller's to forge.
#   a comma list of proxy addresses is also accepted.
TRUST_PROXY=

# ── Card payments (src/payments) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which gateway takes card payments. Implement one from
# src/payments/providers/TEMPLATE.ts and register it in src/payments/index.ts.
#   unset  → `unconfigured`: POST /v1/payments/intent answers 503
#            `payment_provider_unconfigured` and a card order is refused (402).
#            Allowed in production — the wallet and cash still work.
#   mock   → development and tests only. REFUSED AT BOOT in production (its
#            webhook secret is in the repository).
#   a name not registered in src/payments/index.ts → refused at boot, every environment
# The gateway's webhook goes to  POST /v1/payments/webhook/<PAYMENT_PROVIDER>.
PAYMENT_PROVIDER=
# Where a hosted payment page sends the member afterwards. Optional.
PAYMENT_RETURN_URL=
# The gateway's own settings — placeholders for the TEMPLATE. SECRETS: server only.
# PAYMENT_GATEWAY_BASE_URL=https://gateway.example        (sandbox URL in staging)
# PAYMENT_GATEWAY_ENTITY_ID=
# PAYMENT_GATEWAY_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# PAYMENT_GATEWAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Odoo 19 / loyalty server — only used when DATA_SOURCE=odoo (server-side only).
ODOO_BASE_URL=
ODOO_API_KEY=
LOYALTY_BASE_URL=
LOYALTY_TOKEN=

# ── Durable storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset, the server keeps everything in process memory: perfect for `npm run
# dev` and the test suite, and wrong for anything a person typed — a company's
# discount and its 200-person roster are gone on the next restart.
#
# Set it to a Postgres connection string (Supabase's session pooler) and the
# same behaviour runs on a database instead. Both stores are held to ONE
# specification by bff/test/backend-contract.test.ts, which executes every
# assertion against each of them.
#
# REQUIRED in production: the server refuses to boot without it. Apply the
# LOYALTY migrations first, in THIS order (each is re-runnable):
#   20260909_loyalty_backend.sql
#   20260923_loyalty_rls.sql            ← mandatory: closes the tables to anon
#   20260924_idempotency_keys.sql
#   20260925_restrict_financial_history.sql
#   20260926_pos_sales.sql              ← or the till's earn fails on first write
#   20260927_payment_intents.sql        ← or card payments fail on first write
#   20260928_drop_members_plan_columns.sql  ← after deploying a BFF that no longer writes them
#   20260929_pos_point_spends.sql       ← or paying with points at the till fails on first write
#   20260930_pos_sale_refunds.sql       ← or /v1/pos/earn/reverse fails on first write (partial refunds)
#   20261001_referrals.sql              ← BEFORE deploying this BFF: every paid order writes members.first_paid_at
#   20261002_member_transfers.sql       ← or /v1/me/transfers fails on first write
#   20261003_member_profile_fields.sql  ← BEFORE deploying this BFF: every member save writes members.gender
# 🔴 NOT "every file in supabase/migrations": 20260807, 20260808 and 20260810
# belong to the branch-management system's database (warehouse tables, its RPC
# lockdown, branch geo) and must never run against the loyalty database.
# bff/test/lib/schema.ts#LOYALTY_MIGRATIONS is the same list, tested.
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
DATABASE_URL=
# Supabase's pooler presents a chain Node does not carry. 'true' keeps the
# connection encrypted while skipping hostname verification.
DATABASE_SSL=false
